---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import GifAudioToMp3 from '@/components/tools/media/GifAudioToMp3.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="mp3-to-wav">
  <GifAudioToMp3
    client:load
    accept=".mp3,.mp2,audio/mpeg"
    sourceLabel="MP3"
    sourceHint="Decoded to uncompressed PCM at your chosen bit depth. Expect about 10 MB per minute"
    target="wav"
    formatNote="WAV is uncompressed, so sample rate, channel count and bit depth set the output size. The bitrate of the source MP3 does not change it."
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What decoding to WAV does and does not do</h2>
    <p>
      A WAV file is a header followed by raw PCM samples. To convert MP3 to WAV, the tool runs the MP3
      decoder and writes the samples it produces. The result is an exact, lossless copy of the decoded
      audio, but not a copy of the original recording. What the MP3 encoder discarded stays lost.
    </p>
    <p>
      So this conversion does not improve quality. It gives a format that editors, samplers, DAWs and
      analysis tools can open, scrub and process directly, with no decoding on each operation and no
      more quality loss each time you save.
    </p>

    <h2>Choosing a bit depth</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Depth</th><th>Bytes per sample</th><th>Stereo 44.1 kHz size</th><th>Use it when</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>16-bit PCM</td><td>2</td><td>10.1 MB per minute</td><td>Playback, burning, maximum compatibility. The CD standard.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>24-bit PCM</td><td>3</td><td>15.1 MB per minute</td><td>The file is going into a mix where you will apply gain, EQ and effects.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>32-bit float</td><td>4</td><td>20.2 MB per minute</td><td>Your DAW works in float and you want processing headroom that cannot clip.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A 16-bit source converted to 24-bit gets no new information: the extra bits are zeros. It is
      still sometimes the correct choice, because each later processing step in your editor then has
      room below the noise floor.
    </p>

    <h2>Working out the file size in advance</h2>
    <p>
      For uncompressed audio, the size is exact:
    </p>
    <p><code>bytes = sample rate × channels × (bit depth ÷ 8) × seconds</code></p>
    <p>
      A four-minute stereo track at 44 100 Hz and 16-bit is 44 100 × 2 × 2 × 240 = 42 336 000 bytes,
      about 40 MB. The bitrate of the source MP3 is not in that calculation: a 128 kbps MP3 and a
      320 kbps MP3 of the same track give WAV files of the same size. So upload-based converters often
      cap WAV output.
    </p>

    <h2>The gap of silence at the start</h2>
    <p>
      MP3 is a frame-based format: the encoder pads the start of the stream so the first real audio
      starts on a frame boundary, usually 576 or 1105 samples in. Decoders that ignore this play the
      padding as a few milliseconds of silence.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most files written since the mid-2000s have a LAME or iTunes gapless information tag that says
      exactly how much to trim, and FFmpeg uses it. Older or unusually encoded files may not have it,
      and then a few milliseconds remain. That rarely matters, but adjust it by hand if you cut the file
      into exactly timed samples.
    </p>

    <h2>What to do with the WAV once you have it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Sampling and looping: samplers want raw PCM so they can loop at exact sample positions with no decode latency.</li>
      <li>Editing: each cut and save of an MP3 re-encodes it and adds loss. Edit in WAV and export once at the end.</li>
      <li>Voice-over and video work: most NLEs prefer PCM for scrubbing performance and frame-accurate sync.</li>
      <li>Analysis and machine learning: almost all audio libraries expect PCM input and would decode the MP3 themselves.</li>
      <li>Legacy hardware: some announcement systems, telephony platforms and museum installations read only WAV.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Big files, no cap</h2>
    <p>
      A one-hour podcast becomes a WAV of about 600 MB. Here FFmpeg compiled to WebAssembly decodes it in
      your browser tab, so the file does not travel and the only limit is your device’s memory. The
      browser sandbox allows about 2 GB of working memory, which is about three hours of stereo 16-bit
      audio.
    </p>

    <h2>Going the other way</h2>
    <p>
      To make a WAV smaller, or to compress recordings your DAW exported, use
      <a href="/wav-to-mp3">WAV to MP3</a>, which shows the exact size ratio for each file. For a
      lossless archive about half the size of WAV, convert to FLAC in the
      <a href="/audio-converter">general audio converter</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
